@.str.5385 = private unnamed_addr constant [768 x i8] c"  Per RFC sister gaps ML-2, ML-3, ML-5, ML-6, ML-10 (Tensor/ML/Autodiff): tensor_nd is missing 2D matmul (ML-2) and transpose (ML-3) \E2\80\94 workaround: use 3D matmul with batch=1 dim and pre-transpose data manually. SSM rods have no backward / gradient paths (ML-5) \E2\80\94 autodiff via these is forward-only. Quantize lacks FP8 gemv and grouped quantization (ML-6); dequant for int8 / ternary missing (ML-7). Transformer has no causal mask, no encoder-decoder (ML-10) \E2\80\94 only encoder-only attention is implemented. Phase 2b adds the missing primitives. Adopter discipline today: verify each tensor/ML primitive exists before relying on it; check the rod source. Reference: docs/rfcs/gap-analyses/Nucleor_Tensor_ML_Autodiff_Gap_Analysis_and_RFC_2026-05-04.md ML-2/3/5/6/10.\00"
@.str.5386 = private unnamed_addr constant [6 x i8] c"math_\00"
@.str.5387 = private unnamed_addr constant [5 x i8] c"abs(\00"
@.str.5388 = private unnamed_addr constant [9 x i8] c"checked_\00"
@.str.5389 = private unnamed_addr constant [68 x i8] c"info[NUM-G289]: numeric-correctness annotations / calls in source: \00"
@.str.5390 = private unnamed_addr constant [768 x i8] c"  Per RFC sister gaps NUM-G2, NUM-G8, NUM-G9 (Numeric Correctness): math_abs(i64::MIN) silently returns i64::MIN in non-strict mode (NUM-G2 \E2\80\94 `0 - i64::MIN` overflows; math_pow_int and math_gcd inherit the bug). checked_* family uses a single global static int for the overflow flag, NOT thread-safe (NUM-G8 \E2\80\94 concurrent calls race; flag overwritten between call and read). @const_fn attribute is silently ignored \E2\80\94 no comptime evaluation, no error if body contains non-const operations (NUM-G9). Phase 2b: panic-on-saturate for math_abs, thread-local checked flag, real comptime evaluation for @const_fn. Phase 4 v1.0 hard-error promotion. Reference: docs/rfcs/gap-analyses/Nucleor_Numeric_Correctness_Gap_Analysis_and_RFC_2026-05-04.md NUM-G2, NUM-G8, NUM-G9.\00"
@.str.5391 = private unnamed_addr constant [9 x i8] c"clifford\00"
@.str.5392 = private unnamed_addr constant [11 x i8] c"kinematics\00"
@.str.5393 = private unnamed_addr constant [7 x i8] c"frame_\00"
@.str.5394 = private unnamed_addr constant [4 x i8] c"tag\00"
@.str.5395 = private unnamed_addr constant [62 x i8] c"info[QM-7-ROBO-7]: Tier C unvalidated-rod surface in source: \00"
@.str.5396 = private unnamed_addr constant [1373 x i8] c"  Per RFC sister gaps QM-7, ROBO-7 (Tier C correctness): the Clifford-stabilizer rod now has OSS verify coverage for Bell/GHZ entanglement, gate identities, reset/rebuild, a [[5,1,3]] stabilizer-distance smoke, and a rotated Surface-17 d=3 stabilizer/logical smoke; published weight-enumerator validation remains open until the rod exposes enumerator APIs. ROBO-7 frame typing v0838: the compiler-side FRAME-001 phantom-tag mismatch check is live at let-binding sites for `Pose<Frame_*>`-style annotations (`Frame_Unknown` is the migration sentinel; untagged Pose handles still bypass the check by construction). Adopters who opt in get a Mars-Climate-Orbiter-style rejection at compile time instead of a silent runtime miscompute. Phase 2b expands the check across struct-init, function-call, and binop paths and adds broader Clifford weight-enumerator/property invariants; Phase 4 promotes the FRAME-001 surface to v1.0 hard-error across the kinematics / tf / se3 rods. Adopter discipline today: do not treat current Clifford coverage as production proof for arbitrary stabilizer constructions; for frame typing, prefer `Pose<Frame_X>`-tagged annotations over bare `Pose` to opt into the check. Reference: docs/rfcs/gap-analyses/Nucleor_Quantum_Subsystem_Gap_Analysis_and_RFC_2026-05-04.md QM-7 + Nucleor_Robotics_Control_Stack_Gap_Analysis_and_RFC_2026-05-04.md ROBO-7.\00"
@.str.5397 = private unnamed_addr constant [66 x i8] c"info[LAW-G123]: `@law(...)` algebraic-law annotations in source: \00"
@.str.5398 = private unnamed_addr constant [1180 x i8] c"  Per RFC sister gaps LAW-1, LAW-2, LAW-3 (Algebraic Laws): @law(...) annotations are captured and reported, and `nuc test --check-laws` now generates bounded integer checks for low-risk forms (`commutative`, `associative`, `identity`, `absorbing`, `idempotent`, `involution`, `distributive_over`, `inverse`) plus schema hard errors for deprecated aliases, unsupported canonical forms, float/approximate modifiers, and unknown law names. The IR-level `opt_law_rewrite_block` is gated by the `@audit(law_opt_required)` + `@audit(check_laws_passed)` predicate pair (LAW-003 fail-closed when only opt_required is present) AND by the v0845 `law_opt_phase2_proof_validated` IR-level sentinel (always 0 in Phase 1 so no rewrites fire even if both audits are present). Fusion law generation, Arbitrary-driven broad property tests, float-law safeguards (currently LAW-004 fail-closed), and cert-profile SMT proof obligations remain open. Adopter relying on @law(...) for compile-time rewrite still gets no optimization guarantee today; use `nuc test --check-laws` for the shipped check surface. Reference: docs/rfcs/gap-analyses/Nucleor_Algebraic_Laws_Gap_Analysis_and_RFC_2026-05-04.md.\00"
@.str.5399 = private unnamed_addr constant [8 x i8] c"@audit(\00"
@.str.5400 = private unnamed_addr constant [17 x i8] c"law_opt_required\00"
@.str.5401 = private unnamed_addr constant [18 x i8] c"check_laws_passed\00"
@.str.5402 = private unnamed_addr constant [176 x i8] c"info[LAW-AUDIT-GATE]: optimizer rewrite gate opted in (`@audit(law_opt_required)`) and proof-audit attribute present (`@audit(check_laws_passed)`); eligible @law annotations: \00"
@.str.5403 = private unnamed_addr constant [845 x i8] c"  Per RFC sister gap LAW-3 Phase 2-prep (local-claude2 v0842): a source declaring `@audit(law_opt_required)` is asking the optimizer to use its law metadata for low-risk rewrites; the matching `@audit(check_laws_passed)` attribute declares the user has run `nuc test --check-laws` and observed PASS. Today the eligibility report fires; the IR-level rewrite pass `opt_law_rewrite_block` remains a no-op, so no semantics change yet. Phase 2 ships the first concrete rewrite (`f(a, identity) \E2\86\92 a` for `@law(identity = N)` on integer fns) gated on this exact eligibility predicate. Adopter discipline: never add `@audit(check_laws_passed)` without first running `--check-laws` and observing PASS, and never add `@audit(law_opt_required)` to a file whose law family hasn't been audited. Reference: docs/rfcs/v1_PUNCHLIST.md Algebraic Laws Phase 2.\00"
@.str.5404 = private unnamed_addr constant [210 x i8] c"error[LAW-003]: source declares `@audit(law_opt_required)` but is missing `@audit(check_laws_passed)`; run `nuc test --check-laws`, observe PASS, then add the audit attribute before enabling the optimizer gate\00"
@.str.5405 = private unnamed_addr constant [65 x i8] c"nucleor: LAW-003 optimizer rewrite gate without check-laws audit\00"
@.str.5406 = private unnamed_addr constant [6 x i8] c"@cfg(\00"
@.str.5407 = private unnamed_addr constant [8 x i8] c"feature\00"
@.str.5408 = private unnamed_addr constant [53 x i8] c"info[PKG-5]: `@cfg(feature = ...)` gates in source: \00"
@.str.5409 = private unnamed_addr constant [665 x i8] c"  Per RFC sister gap PKG-5 (Module/Packaging): conditional compilation `@cfg(feature = \22X\22)` is parsed but the gate is NOT honored \E2\80\94 the body is always included regardless of the active features set. `--features hardware` to `nuc build` does not gate any code. Phase 2b adds proper feature-gating in the lowering pass; Phase 4 promotes adopter use to a hard requirement (gates that don't match active features become a hard error). Adopter discipline today: do NOT rely on @cfg(feature) for compile-time selection \E2\80\94 use module-level imports or runtime checks. Reference: docs/rfcs/gap-analyses/Nucleor_Module_Packaging_Gap_Analysis_and_RFC_2026-05-04.md PKG-5.\00"
@.str.5410 = private unnamed_addr constant [7 x i8] c"#[dead\00"
