@.str.5417 = private unnamed_addr constant [61 x i8] c"info[RT-G135]: real-time/determinism annotations in source: \00"
@.str.5418 = private unnamed_addr constant [1469 x i8] c"  Per RFC sister gaps RT-G1, RT-G3, RT-G5, RT-G6 (Real-Time / Determinism): #[no_alloc] / #[no_panic] / #[isr] / #[deadline] annotations parse but enforcement has known false-negative surfaces. RT-G1/RT-G3: #[no_alloc] and #[no_panic] scan annotated fn bodies, tagged callees, and bounded same-file helper chains up to depth=8 (v0845 lane 3 / queue 3C raised the no_alloc + no_panic bound from 4 to match the restricts and requires-row depth) whose reachable bodies contain hardcoded allocation/panic patterns. Cross-module flat-namespace `import \22...\22` reach is closed via the resolved-source pre-pass \E2\80\94 imported fn declarations and bodies are textually inlined before `enforce_no_alloc` and `enforce_no_panic` run, so the same RT-001/RT-002 fire whether the allocating/panicking callee lives in the entry source or an imported sibling rod (v0845 lane 3 / queue 3A). Closure escape, fn-pointer indirect calls, and deeper-than-bound helper paths still escape detection. RT-G3 still warns only for integer division/modulo panic risk; arithmetic overflow and array OOB panic sources remain incomplete. RT-G5: #[deadline=N] numeric budget has only runtime check + heuristic RT-004 estimate, not certified WCET. RT-G6: no embedded sysroot \E2\80\94 #[isr] runs on x86_64 host. Adopter discipline today: do NOT rely on these annotations as complete compile-time hard guarantees. Reference: docs/rfcs/gap-analyses/Nucleor_RealTime_Determinism_Gap_Analysis_and_RFC_2026-05-04.md.\00"
@.str.5419 = private unnamed_addr constant [12 x i8] c"concurrency\00"
@.str.5420 = private unnamed_addr constant [8 x i8] c"cancel_\00"
@.str.5421 = private unnamed_addr constant [6 x i8] c"token\00"
@.str.5422 = private unnamed_addr constant [9 x i8] c"channel_\00"
@.str.5423 = private unnamed_addr constant [5 x i8] c"send\00"
@.str.5424 = private unnamed_addr constant [52 x i8] c"info[CONC-G12]: concurrency-rod surface in source: \00"
@.str.5425 = private unnamed_addr constant [638 x i8] c"  Per RFC sister gaps C-1, C-2 (Concurrency): the concurrency rod's `cancel_token` (C-1) is a linker bomb on Linux (extern fn declared, body absent in nucleor_llvm_rt.c POSIX path) and POSIX channel helpers (C-2) are silent no-op stubs that lose all messages. Programs using these on Linux either fail at link time or run with broken concurrency. Phase 2b adds the missing POSIX implementations; Phase 4 enforces a CI gate that builds + smokes a concurrency test on Linux. Adopter discipline today: avoid these helpers if targeting Linux. Reference: docs/rfcs/gap-analyses/Nucleor_Concurrency_Gap_Analysis_and_RFC_2026-05-04.md C-1, C-2.\00"
@.str.5426 = private unnamed_addr constant [6 x i8] c"pure \00"
@.str.5427 = private unnamed_addr constant [58 x i8] c"info[EFF-G123]: effect/capability annotations in source: \00"
@.str.5428 = private unnamed_addr constant [3099 x i8] c"  Per RFC sister gaps E-1, E-2, E-3 (Effect/Capability): direct `pure fn` side effects now fail during `nuc build` with EFF-001 (v0.8.307/v0.8.309), same-file pure calls into user helpers with direct side effects or bounded requires-row violations now fail with EFF-001, pure calls to builtin print-family I/O, undeclared extern/default-unsafe surfaces, and structured scheduling/channel effects now fail with EFF-001, same-file `requires [...]` rows now emit EFF-001 for direct rowed callees, known builtin body effects, and bounded un-rowed helper chains up to depth=8 (v0845 lane 2 raised the requires bound from 3 to match the restricts path), and the RFC-0033 `with [no_alloc]`/`with [Alloc]` subset can emit EFF-003. Block-form `restricts [...] { ... }` now parses cleanly and a same-file pre-pass emits EFF-003 for direct builtin I/O / channel / namespaced alloc-constructor calls, direct calls to user fns whose declared `requires [...]` row overlaps the deny row, AND for un-rowed user fn callees that transitively reach a builtin or rowed callee within depth=8 hops in the same file (R05 Phase 2b first slice + cloud2 delta v0840, local-claude transitive ship v0841/v0843, helper1 v0844 requires/body follow-up). Clean blocks and clean transitive chains compile and run normally. Cross-module flat-namespace `import \22...\22` requires + restricts direct and bounded transitive enforcement is closed via the resolved-source pre-pass \E2\80\94 imported fn declarations and bodies are textually inlined before `enforce_requires_direct_calls` and `enforce_restricts_block_effects` run, so the same diagnostics fire whether the rowed/builtin callee lives in the entry source or an imported sibling rod (v0845 lane 2 probe). Same-file `impl` method effect enforcement also lands via the same path: substring-search on `callee(` accidentally covers `obj.method(...)` call sites, so a method declared with `requires [...]` mismatched against its same-file caller's row, a method whose body reaches a builtin under an incompatible caller row, and a `restricts [...] { obj.method(); }` block reaching method-internal I/O all surface EFF-001/EFF-003 (v0845 lane 2 / queue 2B). The substring approach cannot disambiguate same-name methods on different `impl` blocks; when the source declares a method name twice with distinct rows, enforcement fails open at that name (skipping the row check and falling through to the body walk) so adopter code does not false-positive on the un-rowed impl. Receiver-type-aware resolution for the ambiguous-name case is Phase 4 / RFC-0033 broader effect-row subtyping. The remaining open surface is beyond depth=8 chains, ambiguous-name same-file methods (fail-open since v0845), closures and function-pointer effect capture, broader RFC-0033 effect-row subtyping, and selective `use path::{a, b}` / glob `use path::*` import surfaces (resolver phase 2). Treat unvalidated rows as documentation/audit metadata until Phase 2b effect-row enforcement lands. Reference: docs/rfcs/gap-analyses/Nucleor_Effect_Capability_Gap_Analysis_and_RFC_2026-05-04.md E-2, E-3, E-4, E-9, E-11.\00"
@.str.5429 = private unnamed_addr constant [44 x i8] c"info[T-3-CAST]: `as char` casts in source: \00"
@.str.5430 = private unnamed_addr constant [505 x i8] c"  Per RFC sister gap T-3 (Type System): const-foldable `<int> as char` emits TYP-026 for invalid Unicode scalar values (>0x10FFFF or surrogate range 0xD800-0xDFFF), and non-constant casts now fail closed unless the exact binding is guarded by a recognized scalar-range proof such as `v >= 0 && v < 0xD800` in the same then-branch. The i64-everywhere char ABI remains open until broader char distinctness lands. Reference: docs/rfcs/gap-analyses/Nucleor_Type_System_Gap_Analysis_and_RFC_2026-05-04.md T-3.\00"
@.str.5431 = private unnamed_addr constant [5 x i8] c", ..\00"
