@.str.5433 = private unnamed_addr constant [75 x i8] c"error[MATCH-009]: slice pattern contains more than one dot-dot rest marker\00"
@.str.5434 = private unnamed_addr constant [495 x i8] c"ERROR: slice patterns with rest binding (the dot-dot rest element inside slice match brackets `[a , .. , b]`) are not yet supported in Nucleor. Pre-v0.8.34 surfaced as wrong-class range-expression diagnostic because the parser hit token 58 inside the slice pattern brackets and routed to the range halt. Workaround: rewrite as explicit indexing using vec_len + vec_get. Tracked for forward-roadmap (full slice-pattern matcher with rest binding). Sister to Q1 / Q9 from PARALLEL_AGENT_PUNCHLIST.\00"
@.str.5435 = private unnamed_addr constant [89 x i8] c"nucleor: slice patterns with rest binding not yet supported (see ERROR diagnostic above)\00"
@.str.5436 = private unnamed_addr constant [56 x i8] c"warning[SEND-G6]: HashMap/Cell/RefCell types in build: \00"
@.str.5437 = private unnamed_addr constant [527 x i8] c"  Per RFC-0062 G-6 Phase 3 (v1.0): HashMap<K,V> Sendable propagation is not yet enforced \E2\80\94 the v1.0 contract is Sendable iff K and V are Sendable AND hasher is Sendable. Cell<T> / RefCell<T> are explicit REJECTS for Sendable (interior mutability incompatible with cross-thread). spawn-call sites may silently accept these today \E2\80\94 adopter discipline: avoid spawning with HashMap or Cell/RefCell in the spawned closure capture set. Closure capture-set audit is post-v1.0 hardening. Reference: docs/sendable-inventory.md \C2\A74.\00"
@.str.5438 = private unnamed_addr constant [6 x i8] c"move \00"
@.str.5439 = private unnamed_addr constant [67 x i8] c"warning[SEND-G6-CLOSURE]: `move |...|` capture closures in build: \00"
@.str.5440 = private unnamed_addr constant [519 x i8] c"  Per RFC-0062 G-6 Phase 3 (v1.0, closures): Rust-style explicit-capture closures (`move |args| body`) capture variables from the enclosing scope. The closure is Sendable iff every captured variable is Sendable. spawn-call sites may silently accept closures with non-Sendable captures today. Adopter discipline: at spawn boundaries, prefer concrete types in capture set (`Vec<i64>` over `Vec<T>`, no HashMap/Cell). Per-closure capture-set audit is post-v1.0 hardening. Reference: docs/sendable-inventory.md \C2\A74 case 2.\00"
@.str.5441 = private unnamed_addr constant [8 x i8] c"unsafe \00"
@.str.5442 = private unnamed_addr constant [51 x i8] c"warning[UNSAFE-G7]: `unsafe { }` blocks in build: \00"
@.str.5443 = private unnamed_addr constant [464 x i8] c"  Per RFC-0062 G-7 Phase 3 (v1.0): the OSS compiler self-host source contains zero unsafe blocks; any unsafe block in adopter code is a manual-audit surface. Each unsafe block bypasses borrow-checking, lifetime-tracking, and bounds-check insertion within its body. Adopter discipline: justify each unsafe block in a comment naming the invariant being preserved. `#[allow(unsafe)]` attribute enforcement is post-v1.0 hardening. Reference: RFC-0003 \C2\A7unsafe blocks.\00"
@.str.5444 = private unnamed_addr constant [14 x i8] c"NUC_AUTO_DROP\00"
